                <pb facs="0649" n="541" sameAs="frus:frus1893/pg541"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. White to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">St.
                                Petersburg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-05-19"><hi rendition="#italic">May 19, 1893</hi></date>.
                        (Received June 1.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 97.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my No. 22 and previous
                    dispatches relating to Jacob Goldstein, I have the honor to state that a letter
                    from Mr. Consul Heenan, at Odessa, informs me that the local court at Kharkoff
                    has decided in Goldstein’s favor, but that he “took French leave” last December,
                    has not been since heard of, and that his present whereabouts are unknown,</p>
                <p>I am, sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Andrew D. White.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
